PHASE 3 — CORE IMPLEMENTATION:

Create src/backend/src/index.js (entry point): Server initialization with graceful shutdown, Database connection with retry logic, Middleware stack in correct order, Route mounting, Health check endpoint, Uncaught exception handling

Create src/backend/src/api/routes/health.js: GET /health — Basic liveness, GET /health/ready — Readiness (db, cache checks), GET /health/deep — Deep health (external dependencies)

Implement src/backend/src/config/ with: Environment validation (fail fast on missing vars), Database pooling (size based on concurrency targets), Structured logging (correlation IDs, timestamps, levels), Secrets resolution (environment, vault, or local dev)

Create src/backend/src/utils/errors.js with custom error classes: ValidationError (400), AuthenticationError (401), AuthorizationError (403), NotFoundError (404), ConflictError (409), RateLimitError (429), InternalError (500)

Implement middleware stack: requestId — Attach correlation ID to all requests, logging — Structured request/response logging, rateLimiter — Token bucket or sliding window, requestValidator — Schema validation (Zod, Joi, or class-validator), auth — JWT or session verification, authorization — Permission checking, errorHandler — Centralized error response formatting

PHASE 4 — DATABASE FOUNDATION:

Create initial migration in migrations/001-initial-schema.sql (or ORM equivalent): Users table with secure password storage (bcrypt/Argon2), Sessions or refresh tokens table, Audit log table (who did what, when, from where), All tables have: id (UUID), created_at, updated_at, deleted_at (soft delete), Indexes on foreign keys, frequently queried fields, Row-level security policies (if PostgreSQL)

Implement src/backend/src/models/ with: User model with password hashing hooks, Base model with soft delete, timestamps, serialization, Repository pattern for data access abstraction

PHASE 5 — TESTING INFRASTRUCTURE:

